.TH GLIMT 1 "2026-10-18" "Glimt 0.37.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glimt \- fast, lightweight image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glimt
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE_OR_FOLDER " ...]"
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-remote
.I COMMAND
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-remote\-all
.I COMMAND
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-help
.br
.B glimt
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Glimt
shows the images of a folder, one at a time, with a file list beside them. It is inspired by
.BR geeqie (1)
and uses its keyboard shortcuts, but focuses on speed and little memory: the images on each
side of the shown one are decoded in advance at window size, and folders with hundreds of
thousands of files list as quickly as small ones.
.PP
Given a file, Glimt shows it with the rest of its folder; given a folder, its first image;
given a collection
.RI ( .gqv )
or a plain list of images
.RI ( .txt ", " .m3u :
one path on each line, relative to the list or absolute,
.B #
for comments), those images in their order, the list never written;
given nothing, the folder used last (if that is set in Preferences) or the current folder.
Given several folders, Glimt shows the images of all of them as one list, for example for a
slideshow across them; random order mixes them all.
A URI such as
.I smb://server/share
is mounted with
.BR gio (1)
first.
.PP
It reads JPEG, PNG, WebP, GIF (also animated), TIFF (also with several pages), BMP, ICO,
QOI, PNM (PBM, PGM, PPM, PAM), Radiance HDR (tone-mapped) and TIFF under other names (Zeiss
LSM, gel scans; also grey TIFFs of floats, stretched), and camera RAW files by their embedded
preview. Builds with optional features also read videos, HEIC, AVIF, JPEG XL and SVG. Other
formats (DICOM, FITS, microscope files …) are read through decoder plugins (see FILES).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-slideshow
Start the slideshow once the folder is read. Given a collection
.RI ( .gqv )
or a plain list of images
.RI ( .txt ", " .m3u :
one path on each line, relative to the list or absolute,
.B #
for comments), it plays them in the list's order, for example
.BR "glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-fullscreen show.txt" .
.TP
.B \-\-random
Show the slideshow in random order. Kept for later starts, as in the View menu.
.TP
.B \-\-fullscreen
Start in full screen.
.TP
.B \-\-kiosk
Full screen with only the image, for a picture screen: the keys and the mouse browse, zoom,
change the layout and run the slideshow, but there are no menus or panels, nothing changes
files or settings beyond these, and Escape, Q, Ctrl+Q and closing the window are refused.
Only
.B glimt \-\-remote quit
(or
.BR \-\-remote\-all )
ends it. The desktop's own keys (switching windows, logging out) still work; to keep them
away, run Glimt as the only program of a kiosk session, for example
.B cage \-\- glimt \-\-kiosk \-\-slideshow
.I ~/Pictures
or as the program of a GNOME Kiosk session.
.TP
.B \-\-lock\-settings
For a shared or public computer: Preferences do not open, the sort manager's folders stay as
they are, View \(-> Theme neither saves a theme nor opens a folder or the browser, and nothing
is written to the settings file, so what is changed in the menus (the
theme, the sort order, the zoom mode) lasts only until Glimt closes. Marks, keywords and
collections are kept as always. An administrator can lock every Glimt on the computer by
creating the file
.IR /etc/glimt/lock\-settings ;
in a Flatpak, which does not see the computer's
.IR /etc ,
use the option.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout " LAYOUT"
Show several images at once, as View \(-> Layout:
.B 1
(one image),
.B 2x1
(side by side),
.B 1x2
(above each other) or
.B 2x2
(four). Kept for later starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-fade " off|short|long"
How the slideshow goes from one image to the next: at once, or fading over 0.4 or 1 second.
Kept for later starts.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Name this Glimt: letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ ,
not only digits, at most 64 bytes (fewer in a Flatpak, whose socket path is longer). The
window title shows it, and
.B glimt \-\-name
.I NAME
.B \-\-remote
.I COMMAND
sends
.I COMMAND
to this Glimt only (with
.BR open ,
it starts one with that name if none runs). A named Glimt listens even when remote
control is off in Preferences.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " COMMAND"
Send
.I COMMAND
to the Glimt window used last, on this computer. Exits with 2 for a command Glimt does not
know, and with 1 when no Glimt runs or none answers within 10 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote\-all " COMMAND"
Send
.I COMMAND
to every running Glimt of this user on this computer.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
List the options and remote commands.
.TP
.BI "\-\-check\-config [" FILE ]
Tell what is wrong in the settings file, or in
.IR FILE ,
line by line: a line that is not
.IR setting = value ,
a setting that does not exist or is set twice, and a value that cannot be read or is out of
range, with the value used instead. Prints OK and exits with 0 when nothing is wrong, else
exits with 1, also when the file cannot be read. When it starts, Glimt tells the first of these
in its window, but not a setting it does not know, which a newer Glimt beside it may have
written.
.TP
.BI "\-\-back\-up " FOLDER
Copy Glimt's own folders (settings, marks and stars, collections, keyword tree, plugins and
decoders; not the thumbnails) to a new folder
.I glimt\-backup\-DATE
in
.IR FOLDER ,
as File \(-> Back Up Glimt's Data does, without opening a window: for cron or a systemd
timer. A second backup the same day goes beside the first. Prints where it went; exits with 1
when
.I FOLDER
is no folder or lies inside Glimt's own folders, when there is nothing to back up, or when a
file could not be copied.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH REMOTE COMMANDS
Remote commands reach only the Glimt windows of the same user on the same computer: they go
through a Unix socket in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/glimt ,
which only that user can open. Glimt opens no network port. To control Glimt on another
computer, run the command there, for example
.BR "ssh you@laptop glimt \-\-remote next" .
.TP
.BR next ", " prev ", " first ", " last
Show the next, previous, first or last image.
.TP
.B slideshow
Start or stop the slideshow.
.TP
.B random
Turn random order on or off.
.TP
.B fullscreen
Enter or leave full screen.
.TP
.BI open " PATH"
Show a file, folder or collection. If no Glimt is running, one is started.
.TP
.B quit
Close Glimt.
.TP
.B current
Print the path of the image shown.
.TP
.B selection
Print the paths of the selected files, or of the image shown when none is selected.
.TP
.BI select " PATH ..."
Select these files of the list (at most 100,000) and show the first; print those that are not
in it. Sent right after
.BR open ,
it waits until the folder is read. It only selects: deleting or moving is left to the user.
.PP
.BR current ,
.B selection
and
.B select
print each path followed by a NUL byte, as
.B find \-print0
does, since a file name may hold a line break: read them with
.B xargs \-0
.RB "or " "read \-d ''" ,
not line by line. No remote command deletes, moves or renames a file.
.SH KEYS
The keys are Geeqie's; Help \(-> Keyboard Shortcuts lists them all, and Preferences can change
them. The most used:
.TP
.BR Space ", " "Page Down" ", " \(->
Next image.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " "Page Up" ", " \(<-
Previous image.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
First and last image.
.TP
.BR Z ", " X ", " + ", " \-
Zoom 1:1, zoom to fit, zoom in and out.
.TP
.BR F ", " F11 ", " Esc
Enter and leave full screen.
.TP
.BR S ", " P ", " R
Start or stop the slideshow, pause it, random order.
.TP
.BR ] ", " [
Rotate the view clockwise and counterclockwise.
.TP
.B Delete
Move the selected files, or the shown one, to the trash; in a collection, take them out of it.
.TP
.BR C ", " O
New collection of the selected files; open a collection.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+K ", " Alt+K
The EXIF panel, with the keywords and comment; Alt+K to edit the keywords.
.TP
.B Ctrl+P
Print the selected images, or the shown one, to a printer or a PDF file.
.TP
.B Shift+S
The sort manager: folders that the selected files are copied or moved to with a click.
.TP
.B Ctrl+J
Pan view: the images by day, as a timeline.
.TP
.B D
Find duplicates among the images in the list.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Preferences.
.TP
.B F1
Help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/config
Preferences, as key=value lines (default
.IR ~/.config/glimt/config ).
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/sort-folders
The sort manager's folders, one on each line.
.TP
.I /etc/glimt/lock\-settings
While this file is there (empty will do), every Glimt starts as with
.BR \-\-lock\-settings .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/themes
Own themes, one
.I name.theme
file each, of
.I key=value
lines.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glimt/keywords
The keyword tree: a path such as
.I Places|Norway|Oslo
on each line.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/state
The last folder and the window size and position (default
.IR ~/.local/state/glimt/state ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/marks
Marks and stars, in builds with the marks feature.
.TP
.IB file .xmp
Keywords, comment, alt text and extended description, and (if chosen) stars of
.IR file ,
next to it, read by darktable and digiKam too.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/plugins
Plugins: desktop files whose command runs on the selected files (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/plugins ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/glimt/renames
A Rename Many while it runs, so that one cut short by a crash is put right at the next start.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/decoders
Decoder plugins: desktop files whose command turns a file of a format Glimt does not read itself
into an image on its standard output (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/decoders ).
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/glimt/collections
Collections, as Geeqie .gqv files (default
.IR ~/.local/share/glimt/collections ).
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/thumbnails
Thumbnails, shared with other programs as the freedesktop specification says.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/glimt
The sockets for remote control.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the files above are kept.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures
Show the images in ~/Pictures.
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures/holiday/beach.jpg
Show that image, with the rest of its folder to browse.
.TP
.B glimt ~/Pictures/2024 ~/Pictures/2025
Both folders as one list, for one slideshow across them.
.TP
.B glimt smb://nas/photos
A folder on a network share, mounted with gio first.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-layout 2x2 ~/Pictures
Four images at once.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-random \-\-fullscreen ~/Pictures
A slideshow in random order on the whole screen.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-slideshow \-\-fullscreen \-\-fade long ~/show.txt
The images listed in show.txt, one path on each line, in that order, fading from one to the next.
.TP
.B printf '%s\en' ~/Pictures/*.jpg > ~/show.txt
Make such a list of a folder, to put in the order you want in an editor.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-kiosk \-\-slideshow ~/Pictures
A picture frame: only the image, nothing that changes files; ends with glimt \-\-remote quit.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-name left ~/a & glimt \-\-name right ~/b
Two windows that scripts can tell apart: glimt \-\-name left \-\-remote next reaches the first only.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote next
Go to the next image in the Glimt used last, for example from a desktop shortcut.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote open ~/Pictures/holiday
Show another folder in the running Glimt, or start one.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote select ~/Pictures/*sunset*
Select those images in the list; the ones not in it are printed.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote selection | xargs \-0 cp \-t ~/best
Copy the selected images to ~/best, also when a name holds a space or a line break.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote current | xargs \-0 exiftool \-Model
The camera of the image shown.
.TP
.B glimt \-\-remote\-all quit
Close every Glimt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR geeqie (1),
.BR gio (1),
.BR lp (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.PP
